# Credentials file for the lead-triage backend — EXAMPLE (safe to commit).
#
# The real file is `users.txt` in this directory. It is git-ignored and must
# NEVER be committed (backend/tests/test_commit_guard.py fails the suite if it
# is). In production, point APP_USERS_FILE at wherever the secret store mounts
# the real file.
#
# Format — one user per line, three colon-separated fields:
#
#   username:password-hash:team
#
#   - username and team are case-insensitive; surrounding spaces are ignored
#   - password-hash is the self-describing PBKDF2 form printed by
#     `cd backend && python -m app.password <password>`
#   - lines starting with '#' and blank lines are ignored
#   - a malformed line (wrong field count, empty field) is skipped, never fatal
#   - a line WITHOUT a team is not a valid credential
#
# Teams: every user belongs to exactly one team; teammates share one lead
# board. The FIRST team listed in the file inherits tenant 1 — the existing
# lead board — so keep the original team's users at the top.
#
# The hashes below are fake placeholders — replace them with real output from
# `python -m app.password`.
alice:pbkdf2_sha256$240000$EXAMPLEsaltEXAMPLEsalt==$EXAMPLEhashEXAMPLEhashEXAMPLEhashEXAMPLEha=:acme-team
bob:pbkdf2_sha256$240000$EXAMPLEsaltEXAMPLEsalt==$EXAMPLEhashEXAMPLEhashEXAMPLEhashEXAMPLEha=:acme-team
